     /book/chapter render + reader suppression (loop 17), run-button
     click-eval (loop 18), book/verify hole render
     (scripts/smoke_book.py), export/print order parity (borge loop
     12). course-documents verified 2026-08-31 (P3.1, loop 23) —
     /book/course/cs-interpreters + the compose-never-copy smoke; all
     five top-level smokes now hold.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-29 authored from the cs-curriculum design session
     (docs/cs-curriculum.md). Probe of `borge book print .` on the
     post-pivot tree: chapters land p788+ in readdir order, inline not
     honored, libs/*.pp + migrations/*.sql absent. This chapter specced
     before any implementation; borge repo workstream described in
     .borg-workstream below.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section habitat-book

  (doc "One spec, two projections: borg files stay the source of truth;
        borge renders them to paper; the habitat renders them to live,
        evaluable pages. Courses are compositions over chapters, never
        copies of them.")
  (status implemented)

  (verify
   (build "dune build image")
   (smoke "(load-library \"book\") then GET /book shows borg chapter index")
   (smoke "GET /book/chapter/philosophy renders doc prose with statuses suppressed in reader mode")
   (smoke "nopales example block renders with a run button; clicking evals in the reader's session and shows the result")
   (smoke "book/verify re-evals all nopales examples in a chapter; a stale expected value renders as a hole")
   (smoke "borge book export emits the same chapter order as borge book print"))

  (details
   (doc (|
        WHY

        The borge book is the spec AND the intended CS textbook
        (docs/cs-curriculum.md). The paper pipeline renders files, not
        the literate tree: post-pivot chapters print on p788+ in
        readdir order, the Nopales product layer and SQL never appear,
        and clone history changes your book's TOC. Meanwhile the
        habitat has /book (spec docs, package layer) but the .borg
        chapters — the actual course readings — are not on the habitat
        at all, so no agent, flashcard, or route can reach them.

        Five decisions (all locked unless reversed with an obituary in
        spec/<when ported>):

        1. THE SPEC IS THE BOOK. No forked textbook document. If a
           chapter is unreadable as teaching prose, the chapter is
           wrong — write it better. Register is a RENDER MODE
           (workshop: statuses/verify/agent-notes visible; reader:
           suppressed), not a document fork. Drift stays visible
           (holes), workflow scaffolding stays toggleable.

        2. GIT STAYS AUTHORITATIVE for .borg. Habitat is a READ-
           PROJECTION (seeded rows) of chapter content. Edit-in-
           habitat (chapter editing, marginals, propose-a-patch) is
           a v2 question, explicitly deferred — same reserve as
           habitat-workflow.book-view's "two books merge" line, now
           upgraded from open question to named v2.

        3. ONE PARSER, TWO PROJECTIONS. borge (lib/lang) remains the
           only parser of .borg. `borge book print` and the new
           `borge book export` share one inline-aware ordered walk and
           one example-fence convention; paper and habitat can never
           disagree about chapter content or order.

        4. HABITAT-FIRST EVAL. The course teaches by running code, and
           the habitat is the natural runtime for Nopales examples:
           read-time eval in the reader's session, render-time
           verification over the whole book. OCaml examples are
           capture-at-render-time only in v1 (borge runs the build
           harness; habitat displays block + captured output; no live
           OCaml evaluator — a later substrate decision, not a
           prerequisite).

        5. COURSES COMPOSE, NEVER COPY. A course is a typed outline
           document that ORDERS chapters (front matter -> chapters ->
           labs) and references them; a chapter lives in one place and
           appears in many courses. Per-course paper = a course root
           borg file consumed by the same two projections.
   |)))

  (subsection export-projection
   (doc "borge-side render stream: ordered inline-tree walk, reader/workshop modes, JSON/sexp export artifact — the habitat's feed and the paper's spine")
   (status implemented)
   (implements scripts/verify_book_inline.sh scripts/verify_borg_parse.sh)
   (verify
    (smoke "`borge book export .` writes <stem>.book.json with chapters in inline order")
    (smoke "export includes .pp package sources and migrations as code chapters")
    (smoke "reader vs workshop mode differ only in scaffolding blocks")
    (smoke "two fresh clones produce byte-identical exports (deterministic walk)"))
   (details (doc (|
        BORGE-TOOL WORKSTREAM (lives in the borge repo; requirements
        authoritative here):

        - INLINE-AWARE WALK. The root project's inline tree defines
          chapter order (`pricklypear.borg` -> borge/*.borg). Files
          not referenced by the walk sort lexicographically AFTER the
          inline tree (appendix), never readdir order. `borge book
          print` takes `--root FILE` to select a subtree (course
          roots, single-chapter prints).
        - DETERMINISM. Byte-identical PDFs/exports across clones
          (probe showed readdir-order chaos; checksum the manifest).
        - TYPE COVERAGE. .pp (Nopales product layer) and .sql
          (migrations) become printable chapters.
        - MODES. reader (strip status/verify/agent-note scaffolding)
          vs workshop (today's everything). Mode is a render flag;
          both carry drift holes.
        - EXPORT. `borge book export [DIR] -o <stem>` emits a JSON
          artifact: [{root, chapters:[{slug, title, nodes:[...],
          examples:[{lang, src, expected?}]}], code-chapters:[...]}].
          Same walk as print. This artifact feeds the habitat loader.
        - EXAMPLE FENCES. doc-block convention (shared print/web):
          ```nopales / ```ocaml fenced blocks, optional `expect =>`
          trailer line for verification. Parser extracts them into
          structured example records; prose keeps the raw text.
   |))))

  (subsection habitat-loader
   (doc "seed the export into the one tree: chapters as typed nodes, refresh on release — reads then inherit branch/lens semantics for free")
   (status implemented)
   (implements libs/book/lib.pp scripts/load-book.sh scripts/load_book.py)
   (verify
    (smoke "scripts/load-book.sh <stem>.book.json upserts book chapters without clobbering hand edits elsewhere")
    (smoke "(load-library \"book\") renders an index from tree rows, not from files"))
   (details (doc (|
        - Loader: release-time. public_artifacts/build-release run
          `borge book export` and a small loader seeds chapter nodes
          (type `book-chapter`) under system/main. Additive upsert,
          same discipline as package seed (branching.seed-policy):
          never overwrite after manual edits land — book rows are
          seed, not live authority.
        - Shape: one node per borg SECTION/SUBSECTION (title/body/
          status/mode-flags), one per code chapter (path + source),
          one per example (lang/src/expected). Children ordered by
          the export's inline order — the tree's ordering semantics
          carry the book's chapter order (tree-time).
        - Offline dev: loader refreshable by hand
          (scripts/load-book.sh); habitat never require borge at
          runtime.
   |))))

  (subsection web-renderer
   (doc "Nopales package `book`: /book index of borg chapters + package specs; /book/chapter/<slug> pretty-printed with references, example blocks, reader/workshop toggle")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-30 all four smokes verified. Loops 17+20:
       /book index lists the 34 borg chapters in export order + the
       spec + code corpora; chapter pages render headings/prose/sections
       from tree rows; reader strips statuses + verify blocks, workshop
       shows both. Loop 23 (P3.1): \"course groupings\" verified — /book
       lists the Courses section (notes kind=course via tree/find-by)
       and a second throwaway course renders the SAME chapter by
       reference with zero tree duplication.
       Kernel delta rode along in P2.2: prose-tokens/render-tokens
       one-scan prims + string-split promotion (budget, see libs/book
       obituary).|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "GET /book lists chapters in exported order with course groupings")
    (smoke "chapter page renders headings/prose/code/examples from tree rows")
    (smoke "binding tokens in prose link to /binding/<name>; missing ones hole-red")
    (smoke "?mode=workshop shows statuses and verify blocks; default hides them"))
   (details (doc (|
        - ROUTES. /book becomes the index of BOTH corpora: borg
          chapters (new) and package spec docs (existing
          spec/book-page). Borg chapters at /book/chapter/<slug>;
          package docs keep /book/pkg/<pkg> alias of current
          /book/<pkg> behavior (kernel fallthrough preserved; no
          breakage of habitat-workflow.book-view smokes).
        - SURFACE. pure-html + the ui/layout package; headings and
          prose from section nodes, code chapters as syntax blocks,
          examples as runnable blocks (eval-examples below). Elegant
          + phone-honest before clever: print-considerate CSS is a
          free paper fallback until borge course roots land.
        - REFERENCES. reuse spec-book-page's binding inline+hole
          machinery for tokens found in prose — the drift renderer is
          already proven; the borg book inherits it.
        - MODES. default reader; workshop via ?mode=workshop or the
          /book UI toggle. The toggle IS the "spec vs textbook"
          argument made tangible.
   |))))

  (subsection eval-examples
   (doc "read-time eval of nopales example blocks in the reader's session; restricted prim table; (book/verify) as the book's self-test")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/example_eval.ml libs/waves/lib.pp scripts/smoke_book.py)
   (implements image/lib/example_eval.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-30 all three smokes verified: POST
       /book/eval-example returns value + elapsed and the run button is
       live (loop 18); define refused at the static gate, create/delete
       refused via known_fn capability, tree/upsert fails closed at the
       restricted rim; (book/verify) hole mechanics proven by
       scripts/smoke_book.py on a smoke_verify chapter — fail,
       refusal, and fix-forward all hole — and whole-book verify is
       green at the current 0 real example fences. Example fences in
       the .borg chapters themselves remain unwritten (authoring work,
       not machinery).|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "clicking a nopales block evals it and renders value + elapsed")
    (smoke "blocks cannot define/persist: define/route/create prims are absent from the example table")
    (smoke "(book/verify \"philosophy\") re-evals every block; mismatched `expect` shows as a hole row"))
   (details (doc (|
        - PRIM. new kernel prim `(example-eval src)` (name bikeshed):
          parses + evals a nopales source string in a RESTRICTED
          environment: pure prims + read-only DB prims whitelisted;
          mutation families (define/route/checkout/create/update/
          delete/create-user) absent. Runs in the reader's branch
          env (visible defs bound, thunks forceable) so examples can
          touch real functions. Timeout + step cap (recursion/
          complexity guardrails already exist in lisp/).
        - READ-TIME DEFAULT, AUTHOR-TIME VERIFY. Clicking runs in the
          reader's session — habitat-first eval, the course property
          the design requires. `(book/verify [book?])` re-runs all
          blocks with `expect` trailer lines and reports holes; that
          is the book's `dune runtest`, and hooks into the habitat
          smoke suite (scripts).
        - OCAML BLOCKS. rendered as block + captured output from the
          borge export (captured at render time by the build harness
          there); inert buttons in v1, honest label "captured, not
          live".
   |))))

  (subsection course-documents
   (doc "courses as typed outline documents over chapters: front matter, ordered chapters, lab nodes — compose, never copy")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-31 both smokes verified by scripts/
       smoke_book.py section 8 on the local dev image: course doc
       course/cs-interpreters renders /book/course/cs-interpreters
       with the ordered reading list (document order asserted),
       chapter status badges from the seeded rows, front matter +
       labs sections, reader + workshop registers; a throwaway second
       course referencing the same chapter adds ZERO rows to
       data.book.ch — compose, never copy proven. Course root
       course/cs-interpreters.borg (repo) exports the 4-chapter paper
       via borge book export/print --root; the canonical walk excludes
       course/ roots (borge 9c1d52e). Renderer notes: reading nodes
       reference chapters by a `chapter: <label>` first body line;
       unresolvable refs render as holes; kernel count is
       VList-strict so the run length uses book/course-len (kernel
       issue filed in log/issues).|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "course doc `course/cs-interpreters` renders its ordered reading list with chapter status badges")
    (smoke "same chapter renders inside two courses without duplication in the tree"))
   (details (doc (|
        - A COURSE IS A NOTES/OUTLINE DOCUMENT (`course/<slug>`),
          typed kind, whose nodes reference chapter slugs in order,
          plus front-matter and lab nodes. Renderer
          /book/course/<slug> assembles it; the chapter itself never
          moves. labs link habitat objects (packages, issues,
          /repl snippets).
        - PAPER HANDOFF. a matching `course/cs-<n>.borg` root in the
          repo re-selects the same chapters for borge `--root`
          printing; the export honors it identically, so /book and
          the PDF stay cousins by construction, not convention.
   |))))

  (subsection out-of-scope-v1
   (doc "what this chapter does NOT attempt in v1")
   (status planned)
   (details (doc (|
        - Editing borg chapters from the habitat (write-path; v2
          question alongside the deferred two-books-merge).
        - Live OCaml execution in the habitat (captured output only).
        - Marginia/annotations synced between paper and habitat.
        - Migrating spec/<pkg> package docs INTO the borg corpus
          (they stay their own corpus; /book indexes both).
        - Public access: /book stays private; public release is the
          existing /book.pdf face + its reader-mode flavor.
   |))))
 )
)
